  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 202 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nUpper level ridging remains across the region Thursday night and \nthroughout the holiday weekend. This will contribute to continued \nabove average temperatures and limited precipitation potential. \nEnsemble guidance continues to build confidence in the dry start to \nthe weekend, with chances for precipitation exceeding one tenth of \nan inch remaining less than 10 percent through at least Sunday \nevening. The NBM continues to suggest some lower end PoPs, generally \nremaining less than 20 percent, as some upper level shortwaves track \nthrough the upper level flow. This also shows well in ensemble \nguidance, particularly in the European ensembles, which continue to \nsuggest a 20 to 30 percent chance of exceeding one hundredth of an \ninch of precipitation. In general, would expect little to no \nprecipitation over the early weekend, and if anything were to fall, \nexpecting it to be very light. As for temperatures, have seen a \nslight upward trend, especially for Friday and Saturday, as most of \nthe region is now expected to see highs in the 90s. This would push \nmost of the region at or above the 90th percentile compared to local \nclimatology. In other words, unusually hot for early September, \nwhen our typical highs would be around 84 degrees. In fact, Friday \nand Saturday could see highs 10 to 15 degrees above our usual highs. \nNot too confident that we will be pushing towards record highs at \nthis time, as records remain between 101-106. However, those with \noutdoor plans this holiday weekend will still want to have plans to \nbeat the heat. \n\nLatest forecast guidance begins to show a shift in the pattern \nemerging by the late weekend. Previous forecast runs were beginning \nto build some uncertainty in the forecast for early week, and latest \nguidance may begin to introduce this uncertainty as early as Sunday \nafternoon. While most solutions remained bullish on upper level \nridging remaining across the region, there was some suggestion of a \nmore progressive solution emerging, bringing a trough into the \nregion a bit more quickly than expected. This latest run continues \nto build some steam for the more progressive pattern, potentially \nbringing an upper level trough across the Intermountain West as early \nas Sunday. If this were to occur, we may expect some stronger upper \nlevel flow to track across western portions of the region on Sunday \nafternoon. This would help lift a surface system across the west, \npotentially bringing precipitation chances as early as Sunday \nafternoon and evening. This has also showed a bit more strongly in \nensemble guidance, which now begins to suggest higher chances for at \nleast one tenth of an inch of precipitation Sunday evening. Whereas \nmost of the weekend ensemble guidance suggests less than a 10 \npercent chance, Sunday evening, we now see a 20 to 40 percent chance \nacross most of the region, with the European solutions even \nsuggesting chances as high as 50 percent in some locations. \n\nAs guidance now begins to favor this more progressive solution, \nexpecting that we may see daily precipitation chances early next \nweek, especially as the trough is expected to lift northeast across \nthe Central and Northern Plains. This may also favor some slightly \ncooler temperatures, with highs still remaining slightly above \nseasonal average. This would place highs generally in the mid 80s to \nlower 90s, but there still remains some uncertainty on how strong \nthe remaining ridging will be, especially with a trough expected to \nmove across the region. Will continue to monitor forecast trends for \nthis period, especially as guidance comes into better consensus on \nthe upper level pattern evolution.",
